 "drivingQuestion": "What does 'ruined herself' mean in the mishna of a woman the court permitted to marry?",
 "outcome": "inconclusive",
 "outcomeNote": "Two readings recorded; elaborated in the next unit.",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "yevamot.92a.8",
   "daf": "92a",
   "sentenceId": 7,
   "he": "הוֹרוּהָ בֵּית דִּין לְהִנָּשֵׂא כּוּ׳. מַאי ״קִלְקְלָה״? רַבִּי אֶלְיעָזָר אוֹמֵר: זִינְּתָה. רַבִּי יוֹחָנָן אָמַר: אַלְמָנָה לְכֹהֵן גָּדוֹל, גְּרוּשָׁה וַחֲלוּצָה לְכֹהֵן הֶדְיוֹט.",
   "en": "§ The mishna taught that if «the court instructed her to marry,» and she went and ruined herself, she is liable to bring an offering. The Gemara asks: «What is» the meaning of: «Ruined herself? Rabbi Eliezer says: She engaged in licentious sexual relations» with a man, i.e., intercourse not for the purpose of marriage. «Rabbi Yoḥanan said:» It means that she married in a prohibited manner, e.g., «a widow to a High Priest,» or «a divorcée or a yevama who» had «performed ḥalitza [ḥalutza] to a common priest.»"
  }
 ],
